Title:
DriverStuff (Windows Kernel Driver Exploits, Unprotect/EoP, and Keylogger Samples)

Type:
GitHub Tool

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
- This repository is a collection of Windows kernel-mode driver projects and exploit code focused on process “unprotection,” privilege escalation, and input capture.  
- It includes HEVD (HackSys Extreme Vulnerable Driver) exploit samples (an Arbitrary Write exploit used to remove process protections, and a UaF PoC that intentionally BSODs).  
- It also contains a modified ThrottleStop driver exploit that, instead of classic EoP, targets unprotecting LSASS (with mention of an optional DSE-disable variant when HVCI is off).  
- The “unprotect” driver exposes IOCTLs to remove protection from arbitrary processes and to elevate privileges.  
- A kernel keylogger sample (CTF-oriented) demonstrates keyboard input interception via a read completion routine, with the author noting it’s likely EDR-detectable.  
- Overall it’s most useful for red teamers, exploit dev learners, and defenders studying common kernel abuse patterns (IOCTL abuse, arbitrary write, process protection tampering).

Technical Focus:
- Windows kernel driver development and IOCTL interfaces
- HEVD exploitation (Arbitrary Write, Use-After-Free PoC)
- Process protection removal / tampering (e.g., “unprotect” targets like LSASS)
- Privilege escalation via kernel primitives
- Keyboard input interception via IRP/read completion callbacks
- Driver signing enforcement (DSE) / HVCI constraints (mentioned)

Use Cases:
- Building and testing kernel exploit primitives against HEVD in a lab
- Studying how arbitrary write can be repurposed to disable/alter process protections
- Demonstrating LSASS targeting workflows (credential access enablement via unprotect)
- Creating detection content for suspicious driver IOCTL usage and process-protection changes
- CTF/training scenarios for kernel keylogging and IRP completion handling

Keywords:
Windows kernel, driver, IOCTL, HEVD, HackSys Extreme Vulnerable Driver, arbitrary write, use-after-free, UaF, privilege escalation, EoP, LSASS, process protection, PPL, unprotect, ThrottleStop, DSE, HVCI, keylogger, IRP, OnReadCompletion